      "lineNbr": 44,
      "author": {
        "id": 1314845
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-02-14T23:27:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e If multiple threads are running, I believe, the Zephyr OS will move to other threads.\n\n\u003e As non-essential.\n\n\u003e The newlib Zephyr hooks just do while(1), so I guess the watchdog should reboot.\n\nHmm, so I guess the question is whether it makes sense to override the `exit()` implementation at all then. In EC, the reason I made `exit()` reboot was because we knew we never wanted to call `exit()` in our code and it was an error if we did. IIRC, the upstream implementation of `exit` was just `while(1)`. I haven\u0027t looked at how the watchdog or thread scheduling is set up, but @dawidn@google.com\u0027s comment suggests that it might trigger a watchdog reboot? (Which would mean it\u0027s already doing what we want.)",
      "parentUuid": "5af28d47_ce43c10d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 44,
        "startChar": 22,
        "endLine": 44,
        "endChar": 47
      },
